{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>Take the sum of the sons of Gershon.</b> He should have counted the sons of Gershon the firstborn first. And what they wanted to fix in Midrash Rabbati (6:1), saying that because the burden of the Ark was assigned to Kehat, therefore he counted him first for the honor of the Ark — that itself is difficult: why did He not assign the burden of the Ark to the firstborn, to honor him with the preeminent dignity and might of the Torah, for which the law of the firstborn rightfully belongs?" "And what seems close to me to say regarding this is that the Holy One, blessed be He, wanted to show that the honor of the sages they shall inherit, in order to teach the people that they should honor those who learn Torah and to call the sanctified of HaShem honored, to sanctify him in every matter of holiness — just as He counted Kehat first on account of the burden of the word of HaShah that was with him. And had He given the Ark to Gershon the firstborn, I would say that it was on account of the firstborn status that He counted him first, and I would not attribute the significance to the burden of the Ark. Therefore He assigned the Ark to Kehat and counted him first, and everyone would know to give honor to the Torah and to those who learn it. But since the author of the Midrash attached this matter to the verse ״יְקָרָה הִיא מִפְּנִינִים״ (Proverbs 3:15), as is found in Rabbati, surely his intention is to indicate that the crown of Torah is available to all, and therefore the Ark was not given to the firstborn, so that his glory not lead him to say 'I am worthy of the crown of Torah more than my fellow,' but rather all are equal in it. Therefore it says יְקָרָה הִיא מִפְּנִינִים, that is, more than the High Priest who enters within the innermost sanctuary, for the crown of priesthood only one who is of the seed of Aaron merits, but the crown of Torah is available to all and all may attain it. And וִיקָרָה הִיא מִפְּנִינִים — more than the crown of kingship, which has gold and an abundance of pearls, and none merits it except one who is of the seed of David, but for the crown of Torah all may attain it. And for this reason he expounded in Rabbati ״יְקָרָה הִיא מִפְּנִינִים״ — more than the firstborn status, as it says ״וְזֹאת לְפָנִים בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Ruth 4:7), for the firstborn status none merits except one who is born first, but for the Torah all may attain it. Another matter: therefore the burden of the Ark was not given to the firstborn, so that his mind not become too swollen with pride over it. And some say the reason is because from Kehat came forth Moses and Aaron, receivers of the Torah." "<b>they too.</b> With these two words he sealed the matter, to say that in any event he must count the sons of Kehat first and only afterward count and number the sons of Gershon 'they too.' And were this verse not written, it would be possible to say that the will of the Blessed One was to count the sons of Gershon first because he is the firstborn, and what He commanded first — that he number the sons of Kehat — was not in order to precede him in the counting but rather on account of the importance of the Ark he mentioned him first, for thus we find regarding the Mishkan and its vessels that at the head of all He said: ״וְעָשׂוּ אֲרוֹן עֲצֵי שִׁטִּים״ (Exodus 25:10), and afterward He mentioned the Mishkan. And in their making, Betzalel made the Mishkan first and afterward the vessels. If so, surely the fact that He mentioned the Ark first was not in order to precede it in making, but for the honor of the Ark and its importance He preceded it. So too I would say that for the honor of the Ark, which was the burden of the sons of Kehat, He preceded the counting of the sons of Kehat first, but in truth when Moses comes to lift their head he should count the firstborn according to his firstborn status first — Scripture therefore says: ״נָשֹׂא אֶת רֹאשׁ בְּנֵי גֵרְשׁוֹן גַּם הֵם״, meaning that first he shall lift the head of the sons of Kehat and afterward the head of the sons of Gershon, for every 'גַּם' (also) comes to add to a preceding matter." "And the expression of 'lifting' (נָשֹׂא) is used for the sons of Kehat and for the sons of Gershon, but not for the sons of Merari, because both of them had a preeminent distinction over the sons of Merari: Kehat on account of the burden of the Ark, and Gershon on account of the firstborn status. And furthermore, his burden was more sacred than the burden of the sons of Merari, which was the least of them all; therefore he did not use the expression of 'lifting' for him. And with this the matter of his saying ״גַּם הֵם״ (they too) rather than ״גַּם אוֹתָם״ (also them) also fits well — rather, he means to say: behold, they too have a virtue by virtue of which they are worthy of the lifting of the head, and that is the firstborn status as stated. And for this reason it says in every place ״לְמִשְׁפְּחֹתָם לְבֵית אֲבֹתָם״ (by their families, by their fathers' houses), except for the sons of Levi, regarding whom it says ״פְּקֹד אֶת בְּנֵי לֵוִי לְבֵית אֲבֹתָם לְמִשְׁפְּחֹתָם״ (Numbers 3:15) — and here it says ״נָשֹׂא אֶת רֹאשׁ בְּנֵי גֵרְשׁוֹן גַּם הֵם לְבֵית אֲבֹתָם לְמִשְׁפְּחֹתָם״ — one reason for both, for the firstborn status comes to a person from the side of his fathers if he is his strength and the first of his might. And when it came to exchange the Levites for the firstborns, he mentioned regarding the Levites ״לְבֵית אֲבֹתָם״ as if they themselves were the firstborns, whose distinction depends on their fathers' house. And so too here it says ״נָשֹׂא אֶת רֹאשׁ בְּנֵי גֵרְשׁוֹן״ the firstborn ״גַּם הֵם״, meaning: they too have a distinction, and what is the distinction? — ״לְבֵית אֲבֹתָם״, that is, the firstborn status that traces back to their fathers' house. And if you wish to say that the precedence of ״לְבֵית אֲבֹתָם״ applies specifically to the firstborns and not to the Levites, then we can say that this is why he mentioned regarding the Levites ״לְבֵית אֲבֹתָם לְמִשְׁפְּחֹתָם״, to indicate that he should also count the firstborns of the Levites — for one might suppose that since it is sufficient for a firstborn that he exempts himself from the redemption, the firstborns of the Levites were not given in exchange for the firstborns of Israel, and if so there is no need to count them for nothing — he therefore informs us: ״פְּקֹד אֶת בְּנֵי לֵוִי לְבֵית אֲבֹתָם״, that even the firstborns, whose distinction comes from their fathers' house, he shall count and number, for even though they are not included in the redemption of the firstborns, nonetheless they are included in the numbering of the service of the Mishkan and its vessels."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {3 "The recurring phrase לְבֵית אֲבֹתָם לְמִשְׁפְּחֹתָם appears in varied word order in different biblical verses; the translation preserves the order as the Kli Yakar quotes each instance. The phrase יִנְחָלוּ in comment 1 echoes the idiom of inheritance and has been rendered accordingly."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:40:02.373399826Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}